Z||000009||BC-GT||08-12-2001||01:00 AM||||I just posted this in the tech forum, but what did you do with(about) the torque convertor lock-up? what about all the other devices controlled by the computer? I'm pretty sure the fuel issues will take care of themselves with the carb but aren't some of the dash components controlled by the computer also?<BR>... sorry for all the Q'S, but I'm just about to get a rebuild and really want to go with a carb if possible.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Formula||08-12-2001||10:15 AM||||the transmission is completly independant of the computer.   all you have to do is hook up the tv cable to the carb.  You also have to splice 2 wires together to get the fuel pump to run when the ignition is turned on.  I went ahead and just left the computer hooked up and left all the wiring taped up in the engine bay incase I want to go back to fuel injection.  <P>to switch to the carb youll need to do some custom work to get the cables to hook up.  Its not too hard to do.  The tv cable needs to be longer, and the throttle cable needs to be shortened.  For an auto youll have to use a spacer for the tv cable to work, this also helps fight vaporlock.  I bought a custom mounting bracket that comes off the base of the carb to hold the throttle and tv cables (advance auto parts).  <P>its raining now so I cant get any pictures of it closed.  The thing sits 7-9 inches above the valve covers (compared to the 3-5 of stock) and sits off to the passenger side, it pretty much comes through the side of the bump.  The "scoop" is a wak cooking device from wal-mart that is temporary until I find a hoodscoop that I want. ||||reg||1
